{
  "feedback": "Do not race this controller as written. It changes only ki (0.05 to 0.10) on top of iteration 6 (kp 2.4, kd 0.8, base_speed 1.2), which is the weakest of the recent controllers. Iteration 6 scored 679.10, below iteration 5 (kp 2.6, kd 0.9, ki 0.05, base_speed 1.2, score 699.16). Iteration 5 is the best raced design. Its oval (20/20) and s-bend (20/20) finishes are the source of most of the score, and the slalom has never finished (0/20 in every iteration). The iteration 6 slalom max_error of 0.0632 is no better than iteration 5 (0.0634) and iteration 4 (0.0618 at kp 2.6, kd 0.8). So lowering kp to 2.4 did not help the slalom, and the controller should return to kp 2.6. Iteration 5 is the right base for an integral test. Keep geometry, base_speed 1.2, sensor_weights and line_loss ('last_direction') exactly as in iterations 5 and 6. The planned ki change is a valid isolated test, and the integral is limited to plus or minus 2, so windup risk at ki 0.10 is small. The point is to see whether a sustained lateral bias on the slalom is reduced by integral action.\n\nRevise so the integral test is run on the best-scoring base. Then read the next race by track. If the oval or s-bend finish rate falls below 0.95, return ki to 0.05 and keep kp 2.6 and kd 0.9. If the slalom max_error falls below 0.06 with any slalom finishes, hold the controller and keep geometry fixed. If the slalom still ends off track at max_error 0.06 or above, ki 0.10 has not removed the bias, and ki should go back to 0.05 before any further gain or geometry change.",
